WEBVTT

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:14.620
NASA's new program for human space exploration is called Constellation.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:20.070
For the first time in a generation, we will be traveling beyond low Earth orbit,

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:26.290
returning to the Moon, and expanding human presence to Mars.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:29.640
Exploration must be taken in steps.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.670
Because we learn from every journey, there is a link between every place we go.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.960
humankind travels to Mars there's a lot of work to be done with every mission we

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.870
get smarter about exploration learning the capabilities of our hardware learning

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:15.510
about the environment building partnerships and ultimately reducing the

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:20.410
risks of spaceflight through our knowledge gained taking our journey in

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:25.200
calculated steps constellation will first travel close to home

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.000
220 miles above the earth to a floating laboratory in space

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.810
called the International Space Station.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:34.350
I'm Pam Melroy.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:37.270
As a space shuttle pilot and then shuttle commander,

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:42.170
I've visited the space station three times.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:46.060
The station is the largest spacecraft ever built.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:49.350
Constructed piece by piece in low Earth orbit,

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.510
it is the greatest engineering feat

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.110
humankind has ever attempted.

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:56.510
The station allows unique scientific research

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:59.700
to take place without the constraints of gravity.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.880
But you might be surprised to discover it also plays an important part in our return

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:14.220
to the moon, and ultimately, humans setting foot on Mars.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.810
The Orion spacecraft is America's next manned spacecraft.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:24.580
So the vehicle is going to be capable of low-Earth orbit operations and also capable of going

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:30.000
beyond low-Earth orbit. This new spacecraft design takes advantage of being able to do

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.560
both. It's a very flexible design.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:41.000
Orion serves a need for a space station. It brings crews back and forth, it brings supplies,

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.590
some supplies back and forth, some critical things with it. But the missions to space

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:49.790
stations serve a big purpose for Orion as well. You don't want to take the new car out

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:55.270
on the long duration road trip the first time out. So from a strictly Orion perspective,

00:02:55.270 --> 00:03:00.000
we get a lot of benefit from these near-Earth, low-Earth orbit missions to try out the

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.000
spacecraft.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:06.500
When we go to a station, virtually all the functions that we need on lunar for Orion

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:07.500
will be exercised.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:12.940
You need power generation, you need thermal control, obviously all the avionics, you need

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:16.760
rendezvous and docking which are critical obviously for a lunar mission where you're

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:21.580
sending a module to the surface and then having to rejoin with it when it returns.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:27.140
So virtually every system that has to be used for lunar is used during those low earth orbit

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:27.910
missions.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:39.000
The normal crew rotation on space station is getting to be right around six months.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.860
You know, crews will sometimes be a little shorter to that, but it's a unique opportunity

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.490
in that the crews do stay for months at a time.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:53.370
As we all know, when you put a human into a gravity-free environment, it's a new environment

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:58.940
for the body, and the body is a very plastic thing and adjusts, and so your bones start

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.000
to lose bone mass.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.890
Additionally, your muscles also become weaker because you're not using them every day to fight gravity as you do here.

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:11.900
And even your ability of the body to control itself from a sensory motor standpoint,

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:18.870
how your brain controls the actions of your body, has all been developed against a gravity environment.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:23.710
When that goes away, your emotions have to be basically reset.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:30.000
The entire human research program that NASA is currently working with was very carefully

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.320
reviewed to focus the efforts during ISS on answering constellation related problems, answering

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:42.550
exploration related problems. For example, we are actively looking at pharmacological

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:47.310
countermeasures to bone loss, to exercise and nutrition as countermeasures to both bone

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:53.640
and muscle loss, and specific types of training for the crew to help manage their sensory

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:57.580
motor problems. And so Station is a unique opportunity to look at both how the human

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:00.000
changes during that time and how as

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.670
As a support organization, you have to adjust and support those people to allow them to

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:08.770
function when they get to the place they want to go.

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:14.480
Building on our experience in low Earth orbit, Orion and its lunar lander Altair will travel

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:18.170
over 200,000 miles to the moon.

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:22.390
The missions will be far more complex than the Apollo lunar program.

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:26.620
Astronauts will be able to land anywhere on the moon, stay longer than the Apollo astronauts

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:30.000
were able to, and explore the moon like never before.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:48.820
Well, the moon is fascinating to certain astronomers and geologists because it's an example of

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:54.450
a terrestrial planet that never got past early stages of development.

00:05:54.450 --> 00:06:00.000
So on the moon, we can look at features and processes that have been preserved for three

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:06.400
three to four billion years, and see things that probably were happening on the Earth,

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.820
but have been erased by all of the erosional processes on the Earth. So the moon, however,

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:17.950
is like a history lesson in a textbook on the early days of the solar system.

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:24.540
The Apollo missions were three-day missions to equatorial regions, and they did do some

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:30.000
scientific exploration of the areas around which they landed, but in the constellation

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.360
program, we're going to the moon to be able to learn how to live there, to stay there,

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:41.030
to use resources that are present to enable our missions and deal with these problems

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:45.160
in a situation where the crew is further away from Earth.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:50.280
The constellation lunar exploration scenarios fall into two different categories.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:55.200
With the Altair lunar lander, we can set a crew of four down anywhere on the surface

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.250
of the moon and sustain them for seven days, whether it's to deploy a telescope or perform

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:00.000
a geologic

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.000
expedition.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:07.470
There's another configuration, another variant, if you will, of the Altair lunar lander,

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:14.000
and that's called a cargo lander, and this Altair cargo version will enable us to deliver

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:20.580
large pieces of a lunar outpost infrastructure, like a big habitat or several pressurized

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:25.070
rovers or a large power generation capability.

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:30.000
All the necessary building blocks of a lunar outpost, and this outpost will sustain a crew

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:33.160
for missions up to 180 days.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:36.580
So the Altair lunar lander will be the mode

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:39.130
by which we bring the crew back and forth.

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:42.590
It will take the crew there, wait for them for their 180 days

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:44.830
day, and then be capable of bringing them back

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:48.150
to the Orion spacecraft in low lunar orbit.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:50.790
The Constellation Lunar Program operations

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:53.970
will really be focusing in on what are the capabilities

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:57.220
and the techniques that we need to develop to master

00:07:57.220 --> 00:08:00.000
in order to reduce the risks associated with the sending

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.840
humans to Mars.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:07.980
As an astronomer, I've always found Mars to be one of the most fascinating planets in

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:10.040
our solar system.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:14.720
Robotic rovers have explored the planet, capturing our imagination and giving us a glimpse at

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:16.400
its many mysteries.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:21.390
Ultimately, the Constellation Mission Plan works towards building on this knowledge and

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:26.270
putting human scientific expeditions on the red planet.

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:30.000
Mars is a planet which is more geologically complex.

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:35.970
And it has had water on it at some time, interacting with it.

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:40.530
And we see huge tectonic features and gigantic volcanoes.

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:43.230
I mean, the Olympus Mons is one of the largest volcanoes

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:46.050
in the solar system, about the size of the state of Arizona.

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:49.480
So these features are new things to investigate.

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:52.720
And of course, there's always the intriguing possibility

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:55.360
that early in the history of Mars, when there was water,

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:58.400
there might have been the early development of life forms

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:00.000
or something precursors to life.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:02.180
Human exploration of Mars is different

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:04.710
than any mission we've conducted before.

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:06.870
During Apollo, the missions were fairly short,

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:10.050
three days there, three days exploring the surface,

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:11.910
and three days coming back.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:13.890
But with human exploration of Mars,

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:15.360
it's much more difficult.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:19.320
It'll take about six months for the crew to get out to Mars.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:20.880
That's about what we do on Space Station

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:23.980
in a crew rotation mission, so we're learning a lot there.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:26.460
Once crews get to Mars, they'll explore the planet

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:30.000
for about 500 days, waiting for the proper alignment between

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.980
and Mars. And when that alignment occurs it'll take about another six months to

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:38.390
come back. So overall it's about a two-and-a-half-year mission. Again six

00:09:38.390 --> 00:09:42.390
months out, a year and a half there, and six months coming back. Future human

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:46.470
exploration of Mars will be a stepwise progression, starting here on Earth,

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:50.910
Earth analogs, learning how to test systems and how things operate.

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:56.020
Constellation systems that we're developing today will feed in very

00:09:56.020 --> 00:10:00.000
nicely to future human exploration of Mars, launching the Orion capsule,

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:04.720
fits in perfectly in terms of delivering crew to low Earth orbit, up to six crew.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:09.120
The space station fits in in terms of being a very vital test bed for human operations

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:10.050
in space.

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:15.350
Future systems that are going to land on the surface of the moon, the habitats, the pressurized

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:22.540
rovers, the power systems, all of those elements will be, if not identical, very similar to

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:25.250
the systems that we'll actually take to Mars.

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:30.000
So everything we're doing today within Constellation has a very strong feed forward

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:35.720
from the space station to the moon all the way to future human exploration of Mars.

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:40.930
We don't have a firm time frame of when we might send humans to Mars, but we know it's

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:43.640
going to be a few decades in the future.

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:49.270
That means that today's children who are just entering elementary school may actually be

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:52.000
the actual astronauts who fly those missions.

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:58.560
So inspiring children to pursue math and science is very vital that we keep that going so those

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:00.000
children can actually be the ones that go there.

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:08.150
You know, that's one of the tasks that NASA has for humanity is to start to extend our presence off this planet.

00:11:08.710 --> 00:11:16.190
Unlike the past where many of the children did not have an expectation that humanity was moving in that direction,

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:19.460
the current generation does. It is part of their culture.

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:26.170
And so I think we'll be well positioned for that as long as we prepare them for all the things that are necessary,

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:30.000
that, you know, we commit to education and preparing them

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.500
for the skills that it's necessary to go there.

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:35.620
I think a lot about the longevity of the Constellation program.

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:37.790
I have a five-year-old daughter.

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:43.790
She has her little Orion mock-up, like many of the children of people that work in Constellation

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:44.680
program.

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:49.800
And so I think a lot about the legacy that we leave in these early missions and how that

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:54.380
will evolve over time and be the space program that my daughter will inherit and her daughter

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:56.560
and son will inherit someday.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.000
I think that's where a lot of the pride in what we do is

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:04.880
is that we feel like we are, in fact, the folks that are keeping that alive for our children.

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:10.450
So we're now looking at a process of human movement out into the solar system,

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:14.750
which has more than just technical implications, it has sociological implications

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:18.690
in terms of technology development, in terms of inspiration of youth,

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:21.610
in terms of demonstrating leadership in the world,

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:28.500
in terms of creating new opportunities for economics and economical development in space

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:30.000
and scientific empathy.

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:38.330
from earth orbit to the moon to Mars and beyond constellation is taking a vision

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:44.080
for space exploration and turning it into a strategic mission plan to extend

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:48.690
our human presence farther than we have in the past constellation will overcome

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:54.750
many challenges make new discoveries push technology and write new chapters in

00:12:54.750 --> 00:13:00.000
space history one destination at a time along every planet

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:04.080
step of our journey, we will not only try to answer the mysteries of our solar

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:09.060
system, but also satisfy our human desire to explore.
